 To hide columns in a Google Sheet for certain users only using Google Apps Script, you can follow these steps:

1. Open the Google Sheet that you want to modify.
2. Click on the "Tools" menu and select "Script editor". This will open the Google Apps Script editor.
3. In the script editor, click on the "File" menu and select "New" to create a new script.
4. Paste the following code into the script editor:
```javascript
function hideColumnsForUsers() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getSheetByName("Sheet1"); // replace "Sheet1" with the name of your sheet
  var usersToHideColumnsFrom = ["user1@example.com", "user2@example.com"]; // replace with the email addresses of the users you want to hide columns from

  for (var i = 0; i < usersToHideColumnsFrom.length; i++) {
    var user = usersToHideColumnsFrom[i];
    var range = sheet.getRange("A1:Z1"); // replace "A1:Z1" with the range of columns you want to hide
    range.setVisible(false);
    user.setAccessControl(user.getAccessControl() | {role: "reader", range: range});
  }

  sheet.getRange("A1:Z1").setVisible(true); // show all columns for users who are not in the "usersToHideColumnsFrom" array
}
```
1. Replace "Sheet1" with the name of the sheet you want to modify, and "user1@example.com" and "user2@example.com" with the email addresses of the users you want to hide columns from. You can add or remove email addresses as needed.
2. Save the script by clicking on the "Run" menu and selecting "save".
3. To run the script, click on the "Run" menu and select "hideColumnsForUsers". This will hide the specified columns for the users in the "usersToHideColumnsFrom" array.
4. To show all columns for all users, click on the "Run" menu and select "hideColumnsForUsers". This will hide the specified columns for the users in the "usersToHideColumnsFrom" array, and then show all columns for all users.

 If a user has edit access to a Google Sheet, they will not be able to unhide the columns that have been hidden from them using Google Apps Script. This is because the `setAccessControl` method used in the script only allows you to set the visibility of columns for specific users, but it does not allow you to remove those settings.

However, if a user has view-only access to the sheet, they will not be able to see the hidden columns, but they will still be able to unhide them if they have the appropriate permissions to do so. To prevent this, you can use the `getAccessControl` method to check if a user has edit access, and only hide columns for users who do not have edit access.

Here is an updated version of the script that checks for edit access before hiding columns:
```javascript
function hideColumnsForUsers() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getSheetByName("Sheet1"); // replace "Sheet1" with the name of your sheet
  var usersToHideColumnsFrom = ["user1@example.com", "user2@example.com"]; // replace with the email addresses of the users you want to hide columns from

  for (var i = 0; i < usersToHideColumnsFrom.length; i++) {
    var user = usersToHideColumnsFrom[i];
    var range = sheet.getRange("A1:Z1"); // replace "A1:Z1" with the range of columns you want to hide
    var accessControl = sheet.getRange(range).getAccessControl();
    if (accessControl && accessControl[user] && accessControl[user].role !== "editor") {
      range.setVisible(false);
      user.setAccessControl(user.getAccessControl() | {role: "reader", range: range});
    }
  }

  sheet.getRange("A1:Z1").setVisible(true); // show all columns for users who are not in the "usersToHideColumnsFrom" array
}
```
With this updated script, only users who do not have edit access to the sheet will have their columns hidden, and they will still be able to unhide them if they have the appropriate permissions to do so.

