 Writing a full video codec in Rust would likely require more than 100 lines of code, as video codecs are complex and involve many components such as compression algorithms, error correction, and motion compensation. However, I can provide you with an example of a simple video codec written in Rust that you can use as a starting point.
```rust
use std::mem::forget;

const MAX_FRAME_SIZE: usize = 1024 * 768;
const BUFFER_SIZE: usize = 10240;
const MAX_NUM_FRAMES: usize = 100;

#[derive(Debug)]
struct Frame {
    data: Vec<u8>,
}

struct VideoWriter {
    frames: Vec<Frame>,
}

impl VideoWriter {
    fn new() -> Self {
        VideoWriter {
            frames: Vec::new(),
        }
    }

    fn add_frame(&mut self, data: &[u8]) {
        self.frames.push(Frame {
            data: data.to_vec(),
        });
    }

    fn save_to_file(&self, file_path: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        let mut file = std::fs::File::create(file_path)?;
        for frame in &self.frames {
            file.write_all(&frame.data)?;
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut video_writer = VideoWriter::new();

    let mut frame_data = Vec::new();

    for i in 0..MAX_NUM_FRAMES {
        let mut data = Vec::new();
        data.push(0);
        for j in 0..BUFFER_SIZE {
            let value = i as u8 * BUFFER_SIZE + j;
            data.push(value);
        }
        video_writer.add_frame(&data);

        if i % 10 == 9 {
            frame_data.append(&mut data);
        }
    }

    let mut file = std::fs::File::create("video.bin").unwrap();
    file.write_all(&frame_data).unwrap();
}
```
This codec is a very simple example that generates a video by adding frames of random data to a buffer. It then saves the video to a file in binary format. This codec is not suitable for real-world use, as it lacks many important features such as compression, error correction, and motion compensation. However, it can serve as a starting point for learning more about video codecs and how they work.

