   Yes, you can hide the port number in the URL by using a reverse proxy. A reverse proxy is a server that sits in front of your Flask application and forwards incoming requests to your application on a different port number. The reverse proxy can then map the incoming requests to port numbers that are more convenient for your users.

To set up a reverse proxy, you'll need to install a reverse proxy server like Nginx or Apache on your system. Once you've installed the reverse proxy server, you'll need to configure it to forward incoming requests to your Flask application on port 8080. Here's an example of what the configuration might look like for Nginx:
```
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name medicalsite;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8080;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }
}
```
This configuration listens for incoming requests on port 80 and forwards them to your Flask application on port 8080. It also sets some proxy headers to allow your Flask application to identify the original client.

Once you've configured the reverse proxy, you can access your Flask application using the URL `medicalsite/`, without the port number. The reverse proxy will handle the request and forward it to your Flask application on port 8080.

Note that setting up a reverse proxy can be a bit more complex than just configuring your `/etc/hosts` file, but it's a more secure and flexible solution for hiding the port number in the URL.

 Yottadb uses a binary format called BSON (Binary JSON) to store data in the database. When a record is updated in the database, Yottadb first checks if the record already exists in the database. If it does, it updates the existing record with the new values. If it doesn't, it creates a new record with the new values.

When a record is updated, Yottadb uses the following steps to commit the update:

1. Yottadb reads the existing record from the database.
2. Yottadb updates the record with the new values.
3. Yottadb writes the updated record back to the database in BSON format.
4. Yottadb checks the write operation for errors. If there are no errors, it commits the update.

Here is an example of how Yottadb updates a record in the database:
```javascript
const yottadb = require('yottadb');

const client = new yottadb();

async function updateRecord(key, value) {
  const record = await client.get(key);
  record.value = value;
  await client.put(key, record);
}

