 You are correct, PlantUML is a markup language used to generate diagrams and other visual representations of code, but it does not automatically generate a dependency graph. You would need to manually define the dependencies between your classes in a PlantUML file in order to generate a dependency graph.

However, PlantUML does have some built-in features that can make it easier to generate a dependency graph. For example, you can use the "inheritance" and "composition" keywords in the PlantUML file to define the relationships between your classes, and then use the "graph" keyword to generate a graph of those relationships.

Here is an example of how you might use PlantUML to generate a dependency graph:
```
@startuml

interface MyInterface {
  method1()
  method2()
}

class MyClass1 {
  implements MyInterface
  method1()
}

class MyClass2 {
  implements MyInterface
  method2()
}

MyClass1 --> MyClass2

@enduml
```
This PlantUML file defines two classes, `MyClass1` and `MyClass2`, that implement a common interface, `MyInterface`. The classes are related via a dependency graph, with `MyClass1` implementing `MyInterface` and `MyClass2` also implementing `MyInterface`. You can then use a PlantUML renderer like Pumlly to generate an image of the dependency graph based on this PlantUML file.

