 To replace webpack in your project with Vite, you will need to update the way you handle `webpack_asset` in your Twig templates. Here's an example of how you could do it:

1. First, you will need to install Vite and its dependencies:
```
npm install vite
```
1. Next, you will need to update your `package.json` file to include a `build` script that will run Vite:
```
"scripts": {
  "build": "vite build"
}
```
1. In your Twig templates, you will need to replace any instances of `webpack_asset` with an `img` tag that includes the `src` attribute set to the URL of the asset you want to include. Here's an example:
```
<img src="{{ webpack_asset('path/to/asset.png') }}" alt="Asset" />
```
1. Finally, you will need to update your webpack configuration file to exclude the directory where your Twig templates are located. This will tell webpack not to bundle the assets in those files. Here's an example:
```
module.exports = {
  // ... other configuration options ...

  module: {
    rules: [
      {
        test: /\.twig$/,
        exclude: /node_modules/,
        use: 'twig-loader'
      }
    ]
  },

  // ... other configuration options ...
}
```
With these changes, your project should now use Vite instead of webpack, and you should be able to handle `webpack_asset` in your Twig templates without any issues.

