  The time complexity of traversing all nodes in a binary search tree with n nodes and printing them in order is O(n).

One way to traverse all nodes in a binary search tree is to use an inorder traversal. In an inorder traversal, the nodes are visited in the order of left, root, right. This means that for each node, the left subtree is visited first, then the node itself, and finally the right subtree.

An inorder traversal can be implemented using a recursive function that visits the left and right subtrees of the current node, and then visits the current node. The overall time complexity of this approach is O(n), since each node is visited exactly once.

Here is an example of an inorder traversal function in C++:
```c++
void inorder(Node* node) {
    if (node == NULL) {
        return;
    }
    inorder(node->left);
    cout << node->val << " ";
    inorder(node->right);
}
```
In this function, `node` is a pointer to the current node in the tree, and `val` is the value of the node. The function first checks if the node is null, and if so, it returns. Otherwise, it recursively calls itself on the left and right subtrees of the current node, and then prints the value of the current node. This process continues until all nodes in the tree have been visited.

